Modifications in this fork (see README for detail):
  - Every property-scoped tool accepts an optional site_url, so one server
    process can cover an entire Search Console account rather than the single
    property named by GSC_SITE_URL. (Addresses upstream issue #9.)
  - New list_properties tool exposing sites.list at runtime.
  - The resolved property is reported in each tool's response metadata.
  - A configured default property is no longer required when callers pass
    site_url explicitly.
  - Renamed from suganthan-gsc-mcp to gsc-mcp-remote.
  - Added a remote HTTP transport (Streamable HTTP) alongside stdio, with
    per-session server instances, shared-bearer-token auth, a health endpoint,
    and container/compose deployment files.
  - generate_report returns markdown inline in remote mode instead of writing
    to the server's filesystem.
  - image_page_audit refuses private/loopback/link-local targets in remote
    mode, re-validating each redirect hop.
  - Added per-user Google sign-in (MCP OAuth authorization server with DCR and
    PKCE, per-user encrypted token vault, per-request user context), so a
    hosted instance serves many users, each seeing only their own properties.
  - Security and resource hardening: corrected SSRF address classification,
    connect-time address validation, bounded fetches, an authorization consent
    interstitial, session and rate limits, a row ceiling on Search Analytics
    pagination, report-path confinement, and user data deletion/export.
  - Serves its own public pages: a service description and a privacy policy,
    which Google's OAuth verification requires on the callback's own domain.
  - Bounded Google API calls, browser-hardening headers on the authorization
    pages, magic-byte format gating before image parsing, and continuous
    replication of the token database.

  - Replaced the image-size dependency with a first-party header parser
    (src/image-dimensions.ts), removing two unpatched denial-of-service
    advisories; no unbounded loops, and dispatch is on the file's own magic
    bytes rather than a server-supplied Content-Type.
